# Topic explanations shown when keywords are clicked
# [category] header, then @field blocks (title, summary, challenges, commands, boot_process)

[python]
@title
Python Programming

@summary
Python is a high-level, interpreted programming language known for its simplicity and versatility. It's widely used in web development, data science, machine learning, automation, and system administration.

@challenges
• Performance optimization for CPU-intensive tasks
• Memory management for large datasets
• Package dependency conflicts
• Version compatibility issues
• Debugging complex asynchronous code

@commands
• pip install package_name - Install Python packages from PyPI
• python -m venv venv_name - Create isolated virtual environment
• python -m pytest tests/ - Run unit tests with pytest framework
• pip freeze > requirements.txt - Generate dependency list for project
• python -c "import sys; print(sys.version)" - Check Python version from command line
• python -m pip install --upgrade pip - Update pip to latest version
• python -m pdb script.py - Debug Python script interactively
• python -m profile script.py - Profile script performance and bottlenecks


[docker]
@title
Docker Containerization

@summary
Docker is a containerization platform that packages applications and their dependencies into lightweight, portable containers. It enables consistent deployment across different environments.

@challenges
• Container security and isolation
• Resource management and optimization
• Networking between containers
• Persistent data storage
• Container orchestration at scale

@commands
• docker build -t image_name . - Build Docker image from Dockerfile in current directory
• docker run -d -p 8080:80 image_name - Run container in background, map port 8080 to 80
• docker ps -a - List all containers (running and stopped)
• docker logs container_id - View container output and error logs
• docker-compose up -d - Start multi-container application in background
• docker exec -it container_id /bin/bash - Get interactive shell in running container
• docker stop container_id - Gracefully stop running container
• docker rm container_id - Remove stopped container from system


[aws]
@title
Amazon Web Services (AWS)

@summary
AWS is a comprehensive cloud computing platform offering over 200 services including compute, storage, databases, networking, and security. It's the leading cloud provider globally.

@challenges
• Cost optimization and resource management
• Security and compliance
• Multi-region deployment
• Service integration complexity
• Monitoring and troubleshooting distributed systems

@commands
• aws s3 ls s3://bucket-name - List objects in S3 bucket
• aws ec2 describe-instances - Show all EC2 instances and their status
• aws lambda list-functions - List all Lambda functions in your account
• aws cloudformation describe-stacks - Show CloudFormation stack information
• aws logs describe-log-groups - List CloudWatch log groups
• aws s3 cp file.txt s3://bucket/ - Upload file to S3 bucket
• aws ec2 start-instances --instance-ids i-1234567890abcdef0 - Start specific EC2 instance
• aws configure list - Check current AWS configuration settings


[linux]
@title
Linux System Administration

@summary
Linux is an open-source Unix-like operating system kernel. It's widely used in servers, embedded systems, and development environments. System administration involves managing users, processes, services, system resources, boot processes, power management, storage, and filesystems.

@challenges
• System security hardening
• Performance tuning and optimization
• Service dependency management
• Log analysis and troubleshooting
• Backup and disaster recovery
• Boot process and bootloader configuration
• Power management and ACPI issues
• Storage and filesystem management
• RAID and LVM configuration
• Kernel and hardware compatibility

@commands
SYSTEM & SERVICES:
• sudo systemctl status service_name - Check if systemd service is running
• sudo systemctl restart service_name - Restart a systemd service
• sudo systemctl enable service_name - Enable service to start on boot
• journalctl -u service_name -f - Monitor systemd service logs
• ps aux | grep process_name - Find running processes by name
• top - Display running processes and system resource usage

BOOT & POWER:
• systemctl poweroff - Shutdown system gracefully
• systemctl reboot - Reboot system
• systemctl suspend - Suspend to RAM
• systemctl hibernate - Hibernate to disk
• dmesg | grep -i error - Check kernel messages for errors
• dmesg | less - Review full kernel boot messages
• journalctl -b - Show boot logs from current boot
• journalctl -b -1 - Show logs from previous boot
• grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg - Regenerate GRUB configuration
• efibootmgr -v - List EFI boot entries
• lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-*.img - List initramfs contents
• mkinitrd or dracut - Rebuild initramfs

STORAGE & FILESYSTEMS:
• df -h - Show disk space usage in human-readable format
• lsblk - List all block devices and partitions
• fdisk -l - List disk partitions
• mount /dev/sda1 /mnt - Mount filesystem
• umount /mnt - Unmount filesystem
• blkid - Show filesystem UUIDs and types
• vgs - Display volume groups
• lvs - Display logical volumes
• pvs - Display physical volumes
• mdadm --detail /dev/md0 - Show RAID array details
• find /path -name "*.log" - Search for files by name pattern

@boot_process
THE LINUX BOOT PROCESS - STAGE BY STAGE:

STAGE 1: BIOS/UEFI FIRMWARE INITIALIZATION
• Power-on self-test (POST) - Hardware diagnostics
• Hardware detection and initialization
• Boot device selection (BIOS: boot order, UEFI: EFI boot entries)
• Locates bootloader on boot device (MBR for BIOS, EFI partition for UEFI)
• Transfers control to bootloader

STAGE 2: BOOTLOADER (GRUB - Grand Unified Bootloader)
• GRUB Stage 1 (BIOS): Loaded from MBR (first 512 bytes)
• GRUB Stage 1.5: Loads filesystem drivers to read /boot
• GRUB Stage 2: Loads GRUB configuration (/boot/grub/grub.cfg)
• Displays boot menu (if multiple kernels/OSes)
• Loads selected kernel and initramfs into memory
• Transfers control to kernel

STAGE 3: KERNEL LOADING & INITIALIZATION
• Kernel decompression (if compressed)
• Kernel initialization:
  - Hardware detection and initialization
  - Memory management setup
  - CPU initialization
  - Device driver loading
  - Mounts root filesystem (initially read-only)
• Kernel starts first user-space process: /sbin/init (PID 1)

STAGE 4: INITRAMFS (INITIAL RAM FILESYSTEM)
• Temporary root filesystem loaded into RAM
• Contains essential drivers and tools
• Mounts real root filesystem:
  - Loads necessary drivers (RAID, LVM, encryption, etc.)
  - Unlocks encrypted volumes
  - Assembles RAID arrays
  - Activates LVM volume groups
  - Mounts root filesystem
• Switches root (pivot_root) to real root filesystem
• Executes /sbin/init from real root

STAGE 5: INIT PROCESS (SYSTEMD/UPSTART/SYSV)
• /sbin/init becomes PID 1 (parent of all processes)
• Systemd (modern init system):
  - Parses /etc/systemd/system/ and /usr/lib/systemd/system/
  - Loads unit files (.service, .target, .mount, etc.)
  - Resolves dependencies
  - Starts target units (default.target = graphical.target or multi-user.target)
  - Parallel service startup
• SysV init (legacy):
  - Runs /etc/inittab
  - Executes scripts in /etc/rc.d/rcS.d/ (S = start)
  - Runs runlevel scripts (/etc/rc.d/rcN.d/)

STAGE 6: SYSTEM INITIALIZATION & SERVICE STARTUP
• Mounts filesystems (/etc/fstab)
• Starts essential services:
  - udev (device management)
  - Network services
  - System logging (rsyslog/journald)
  - Security services (SELinux/AppArmor)
  - Display manager (if graphical)
• Runs startup scripts (/etc/rc.local or systemd units)

STAGE 7: USER SPACE & LOGIN
• Display manager starts (GDM, LightDM, etc.) for graphical
• Or getty processes start for text console
• Login prompt appears
• User authentication
• Shell initialization (/etc/profile, ~/.bashrc, etc.)
• System ready for use

TROUBLESHOOTING BOOT ISSUES:
• Check BIOS/UEFI boot order and settings
• Verify GRUB configuration: /boot/grub/grub.cfg
• Review kernel messages: dmesg | less
• Check initramfs: lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-*.img
• Examine systemd boot logs: journalctl -b
• Verify filesystem: fsck /dev/sda1
• Check fstab: cat /etc/fstab
• Test GRUB: grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg


[git]
@title
Git Version Control

@summary
Git is a distributed version control system that tracks changes in source code during software development. It enables collaboration, branching, merging, and maintaining project history.

@challenges
• Merge conflicts resolution
• Branch management strategies
• Large repository performance
• Access control and permissions
• Backup and disaster recovery

@commands
• git clone repository_url - Download repository to local machine
• git add . && git commit -m "message" - Stage all changes and commit with message
• git push origin branch_name - Upload local commits to remote repository
• git pull origin main - Download and merge changes from remote main branch
• git log --oneline - Show commit history in compact format
• git status - Check which files are modified, staged, or untracked
• git branch -a - List all local and remote branches
• git checkout -b new_branch - Create and switch to new branch


[database]
@title
Database Management

@summary
Databases are organized collections of data that can be easily accessed, managed, and updated. They support various data models including relational, NoSQL, and in-memory databases.

@challenges
• Query optimization and performance
• Data consistency and ACID properties
• Scalability and sharding
• Backup and recovery procedures
• Security and access control

@commands
• SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE condition; - Query data from database table
• CREATE INDEX idx_name ON table_name(column); - Create index to speed up queries
• SHOW PROCESSLIST; - Display currently running database processes
• mysqldump -u user -p database_name > backup.sql - Create database backup file
• EXPLAIN SELECT query; - Analyze query execution plan and performance
• SHOW TABLES; - List all tables in current database
• DESCRIBE table_name; - Show table structure and column information
• GRANT SELECT ON database.* TO user@localhost; - Grant database permissions to user


[networking]
@title
Computer Networking & Network Administration

@summary
Computer networking involves connecting computers and devices to share resources and information. It includes protocols, routing, switching, network security, interface configuration, VLANs, bonding, firewalls, and network troubleshooting.

@challenges
• Network security and firewalls
• Bandwidth optimization
• Latency and packet loss
• Protocol compatibility
• Troubleshooting connectivity issues
• Interface configuration and bonding
• VLAN and network segmentation
• Routing and gateway configuration
• DNS resolution problems
• Network performance tuning

@commands
CONNECTIVITY & DIAGNOSTICS:
• ping hostname_or_ip - Test network connectivity and latency
• traceroute destination - Trace network path to destination
• mtr hostname - Continuous traceroute with statistics
• ss -tulpn - Show listening ports and connections (modern netstat)
• netstat -an - Show all network connections and listening ports
• ip addr show - Display all network interfaces and IP addresses
• ip link show - Show network interface status
• ip route show - Display routing table

PACKET CAPTURE & ANALYSIS:
• tcpdump -i eth0 -n "port 80" - Capture packets on interface
• tcpdump -i any -w capture.pcap - Save packets to file
• wireshark - GUI packet analyzer
• nmap -sS target_ip - Scan target for open ports using SYN scan
• nmap -A target_ip - Aggressive scan with OS detection

INTERFACE CONFIGURATION:
• ip addr add 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0 - Add IP address to interface
• ip link set eth0 up - Bring interface up
• ip link set eth0 down - Bring interface down
• ifconfig eth0 192.168.1.100 netmask 255.255.255.0 - Configure interface (legacy)
• ip link add bond0 type bond - Create network bond interface
• ip link set eth0 master bond0 - Add interface to bond

FIREWALL & SECURITY:
• iptables -L -n -v - List firewall rules
• iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT - Allow SSH
• ufw status - Check UFW firewall status
• ufw allow 80/tcp - Allow HTTP traffic

DNS & REMOTE ACCESS:
• dig domain.com - Query DNS records for domain name
• nslookup domain.com - Lookup domain name
• ssh user@hostname - Connect to remote server securely
• curl -I http://example.com - Check if website is accessible (HEAD request)
• wget http://example.com/file - Download file via HTTP


[security]
@title
Information Security

@summary
Information security involves protecting digital information from unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction. It includes encryption, authentication, and access control.

@challenges
• Threat detection and prevention
• Identity and access management
• Encryption key management
• Compliance and auditing
• Incident response and forensics

@commands
• openssl genrsa -out private.key 2048 - Generate 2048-bit RSA private key
• ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 - Create SSH key pair for secure authentication
• nmap -sV target_ip - Scan target and identify service versions
• fail2ban-client status - Check status of intrusion prevention system
• certbot --nginx -d domain.com - Obtain SSL certificate for domain
• chmod 600 private.key - Set secure permissions (owner read/write only)
• sudo ufw status - Check firewall rules and status
• openssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout - Examine SSL certificate details


[monitoring]
@title
System Monitoring

@summary
System monitoring involves observing and measuring system performance, availability, and health. It includes logging, metrics collection, alerting, and visualization.

@challenges
• Log aggregation and analysis
• Metric collection at scale
• Alert fatigue and noise
• Performance impact of monitoring
• Data retention and storage

@commands
• tail -f /var/log/application.log - Monitor application logs in real-time
• htop - Interactive process viewer with resource usage
• iostat -x 1 - Monitor disk I/O statistics every second
• prometheus --config.file=prometheus.yml - Start metrics collection server
• grafana-server - Launch web-based monitoring dashboard
• journalctl -u service_name -f - Monitor systemd service logs
• free -h - Display memory usage in human-readable format
• sar -u 1 10 - Monitor CPU usage every second for 10 iterations


[ci_cd]
@title
Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment

@summary
CI/CD is a set of practices that automate the integration and deployment of code changes. It includes automated testing, building, and deployment pipelines.

@challenges
• Pipeline complexity and maintenance
• Test environment management
• Deployment rollback strategies
• Security in CI/CD pipelines
• Performance and scalability

@commands
• git push origin feature-branch - Trigger CI pipeline by pushing code
• docker build -t app:latest . - Build application container image
• kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml - Deploy application to Kubernetes cluster
• helm install release-name chart/ - Install application using Helm package manager
• jenkins build job-name - Trigger Jenkins CI/CD pipeline manually
• docker-compose up -d - Start multi-container application stack
• kubectl get pods - List all running pods in Kubernetes cluster
• helm list - Show all installed Helm releases


[automation]
@title
Ansible and Automation Controller

@summary
Ansible is a configuration management and automation platform. Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform (AAP) and Automation Controller (formerly Ansible Tower) provide centralized job templates, inventories, credentials, workflows, and RBAC for enterprise automation at scale.

@challenges
• Controller upgrade planning and rollback
• Execution environment image compatibility
• Credential and secret migration
• Inventory and project synchronization after upgrade
• Job template and workflow job breakage across versions
• Database backup and restore during platform upgrades
• Mixed-version controller/execution node topologies during migration

@commands
BACKUP & UPGRADE PREP:
• awx-manage backup - Create Automation Controller backup (AWX/AAP)
• awx-manage restore /path/to/backup.tar.gz - Restore from backup
• ansible-playbook --version - Verify Ansible core version
• automation-controller --version - Check controller version

INVENTORY & PROJECTS:
• awx-manage inventory_import --source=/path/inventory.ini - Import inventory
• ansible-inventory --list -i inventory.yml - Show parsed inventory
• ansible-galaxy collection install namespace.collection - Install collection

JOB EXECUTION:
• awx job_templates list - List job templates via CLI/API
• ansible-playbook site.yml -i inventory - Run playbook locally
• ansible-navigator run site.yml -i inventory - Run with execution environment

POST-UPGRADE VALIDATION:
• awx-manage check_db - Validate controller database
• curl -k https://controller.example.com/api/v2/ping/ - API health check
• awx-manage list_instances - Verify execution nodes are connected


[hft]
@title
High-Frequency Trading (HFT)

@summary
High-Frequency Trading uses sophisticated algorithms and ultra-fast computer systems to execute trades in milliseconds or microseconds. It relies on speed, low latency, and market data analysis to capitalize on small price differences.

@challenges
• Ultra-low latency requirements (microseconds)
• Market data processing at high speeds
• Risk management in volatile markets
• Regulatory compliance and reporting
• Infrastructure costs and co-location
• Algorithm stability under market stress

@commands
• ping -c 100 exchange-server.com - Test latency to exchange (100 packets)
• tcpdump -i eth0 -n "port 443" - Capture HTTPS traffic on network interface
• strace -p $(pgrep trading_engine) - Trace system calls of trading process
• perf record -g ./trading_algorithm - Profile trading algorithm performance
• netstat -i | grep -E "(RX|TX)" - Monitor network interface statistics
• taskset -c 0-3 ./trading_app - Pin trading app to specific CPU cores
• ethtool -K eth0 gro off tso off - Disable network optimizations for low latency
• numactl --cpunodebind=0 --membind=0 ./app - Bind to specific NUMA node


[hpc]
@title
High-Performance Computing (HPC)

@summary
HPC involves using supercomputers and parallel processing techniques to solve complex computational problems. It includes cluster computing, GPU acceleration, and distributed computing for scientific, engineering, and research applications.

@challenges
• Parallel algorithm design and optimization
• Load balancing across compute nodes
• Memory bandwidth and cache optimization
• Inter-node communication bottlenecks
• Fault tolerance in large-scale systems
• Power consumption and cooling requirements

@commands
• mpirun -np 64 ./parallel_program - Run MPI program on 64 processes
• nvidia-smi -l 1 - Monitor GPU usage every second
• htop -d 1 - Display system resources every second
• sacct -j job_id --format=JobID,State,ExitCode - Check Slurm job status and exit code
• squeue -u username - Show user's jobs in Slurm queue
• srun --gres=gpu:1 --pty bash - Get interactive shell with GPU access
• sinfo -N -l - List all compute nodes and their status
• scontrol show job job_id - Display detailed job information


[network_storage]
@title
Storage & Network Storage Technologies

@summary
Storage systems include local disk management, RAID, LVM, and network storage solutions like NAS (Network Attached Storage), SAN (Storage Area Network), and distributed storage. They enable centralized data management, high availability, and efficient storage utilization.

@challenges
• Network latency and bandwidth optimization
• Data consistency across distributed systems
• Backup and disaster recovery strategies
• Storage capacity planning and scaling
• Security and access control
• Performance tuning for different workloads
• RAID configuration and recovery
• LVM management and thin provisioning
• Filesystem selection and optimization
• Storage performance monitoring

@commands
LOCAL STORAGE:
• lsblk - List all block devices and partitions
• fdisk -l - List disk partitions
• parted -l - List partitions (GPT support)
• df -h - Show disk space usage
• du -sh /path - Show directory size
• iostat -x 1 - Monitor storage I/O performance
• smartctl -a /dev/sda - Check disk health (SMART)

RAID MANAGEMENT:
• mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sda1 /dev/sdb1 - Create RAID1
• mdadm --detail /dev/md0 - Show RAID array details
• mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdc1 - Add disk to RAID
• cat /proc/mdstat - Check RAID status

LVM MANAGEMENT:
• pvs - Display physical volumes
• vgs - Display volume groups
• lvs - Display logical volumes
• pvcreate /dev/sda1 - Create physical volume
• vgcreate vg_name /dev/sda1 - Create volume group
• lvcreate -L 10G -n lv_name vg_name - Create logical volume
• lvextend -L +5G /dev/vg_name/lv_name - Extend logical volume
• resize2fs /dev/vg_name/lv_name - Resize ext4 filesystem

NETWORK STORAGE:
• mount -t nfs server:/path /local/mount - Mount NFS share
• mount -t cifs //server/share /mnt -o username=user - Mount CIFS/SMB share
• iscsiadm -m discovery -t st -p target_ip - Discover iSCSI targets
• iscsiadm -m node -T target_name -p target_ip -l - Login to iSCSI target
• df -h | grep nfs - Check NFS mount points
• showmount -e server - Show NFS exports available
• smbclient -L //server -U username - List SMB/CIFS shares

DISTRIBUTED STORAGE:
• ceph status - Check Ceph cluster health
• ceph df - Show Ceph storage usage
• gluster volume info - Display GlusterFS volume information
• gluster volume status - Check GlusterFS volume status

BACKUP & RESTORE:
• rsync -avz source/ dest/ - Sync directories
• tar -czf backup.tar.gz /path - Create compressed archive
• tar -xzf backup.tar.gz - Extract archive
• dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/sdb bs=4M - Clone disk


[virtualization]
@title
Virtualization & Virtual Machine Management

@summary
Virtualization enables running multiple operating systems on a single physical host through hypervisors like VMware vSphere, KVM, Xen, or Hyper-V. Container technologies like Docker and Kubernetes provide lightweight application virtualization. Virtualization improves resource utilization, enables workload isolation, and simplifies deployment and management.

@challenges
• Resource allocation and overcommitment
• Performance optimization and overhead
• Storage management and datastore optimization
• Network configuration and security
• VM migration and high availability
• Container orchestration and scaling
• Backup and disaster recovery
• License management and compliance
• Troubleshooting performance issues
• Integration with storage and networking

@commands
VMWARE VSPHERE:
• vim-cmd vmsvc/getallvms - List all VMs on ESXi host
• vim-cmd vmsvc/power.on vmid - Power on VM
• vim-cmd vmsvc/power.off vmid - Power off VM
• vim-cmd vmsvc/power.getstate vmid - Check VM power state
• esxcli vm process list - List running VM processes
• esxcli storage vmfs extent list - List VMFS datastores
• esxcli network vswitch standard list - List virtual switches

KVM/QEMU:
• virsh list --all - List all VMs (running and stopped)
• virsh start vm_name - Start VM
• virsh shutdown vm_name - Gracefully shutdown VM
• virsh destroy vm_name - Force stop VM
• virsh dominfo vm_name - Show VM information
• virsh snapshot-create vm_name - Create VM snapshot
• qemu-img create -f qcow2 disk.qcow2 10G - Create virtual disk
• qemu-img convert -O qcow2 source.img dest.qcow2 - Convert disk format

DOCKER:
• docker ps -a - List all containers
• docker images - List all images
• docker run -d -p 8080:80 image_name - Run container
• docker stop container_id - Stop container
• docker exec -it container_id /bin/bash - Get shell in container
• docker logs container_id - View container logs
• docker-compose up -d - Start multi-container application

KUBERNETES:
• kubectl get pods - List all pods
• kubectl get nodes - List cluster nodes
• kubectl describe pod pod_name - Show pod details
• kubectl logs pod_name - View pod logs
• kubectl exec -it pod_name -- /bin/bash - Get shell in pod
• kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml - Deploy application
• kubectl scale deployment name --replicas=3 - Scale deployment


[performance]
@title
Performance Optimization & Quantum Computing

@summary
Performance optimization focuses on achieving maximum throughput and minimum latency in computing systems. This includes FPGA acceleration, RDMA networking, kernel bypass techniques, and hardware-optimized programming for trading, HPC, and real-time applications. Quantum computing represents the next frontier in computational performance, offering exponential speedups for specific problem classes through quantum algorithms and quantum advantage.

@challenges
• Ultra-low latency requirements (nanoseconds to microseconds)
• Hardware-software co-design complexity
• Memory hierarchy optimization and cache efficiency
• Network stack bypass and user-space networking
• FPGA programming and verification
• NUMA topology and CPU affinity management
• Real-time system constraints and determinism
• Quantum error correction and coherence maintenance
• Quantum algorithm design and optimization
• Quantum-classical hybrid system integration

@commands
TRADITIONAL PERFORMANCE:
• numactl --cpunodebind=0 --membind=0 ./app - Bind app to specific NUMA node
• taskset -c 0-3 ./process - Pin process to CPU cores 0-3
• perf record -g -e cycles,instructions ./program - Profile with call graphs
• rdma_cm -s -p 12345 - Start RDMA connection manager on port 12345
• ibv_devinfo -v - Display InfiniBand device information
• fpga-load-accel -a accelerator.bit - Load FPGA accelerator bitstream
• dpdk-testpmd -l 0-3 -n 4 -- -i - Test DPDK packet processing
• ethtool -K eth0 gro off tso off - Disable network optimizations for low latency

QUANTUM COMPUTING:
• qiskit --version - Check IBM Qiskit quantum framework version
• cirq --version - Check Google Cirq quantum framework version
• qiskit-aer --version - Check Qiskit Aer simulator version
• python -c "import qiskit; print(qiskit.__version__)" - Verify Qiskit installation
• qiskit transpile --optimization_level=3 circuit - Optimize quantum circuit
• qiskit execute --shots=1024 circuit - Run quantum circuit with 1024 shots
• qiskit optimize --backend=backend circuit - Optimize for specific quantum backend


[slurm]
@title
Slurm Workload Manager & Open OnDemand

@summary
Slurm (Simple Linux Utility for Resource Management) is an open-source job scheduler for Linux clusters. It manages job queues, allocates resources, and provides job accounting. Open OnDemand is a web-based interface for HPC clusters that integrates with Slurm, providing user-friendly access to cluster resources, job management, and interactive applications.

@challenges
• Job queue management and priority handling
• Resource allocation and scheduling optimization
• Job dependency and workflow management
• Fair share and user/group quotas
• Node failure handling and job recovery
• Performance monitoring and accounting
• Integration with storage and network systems
• Web interface configuration and security
• Interactive application management
• User authentication and authorization

@commands
SLURM COMMANDS:
• squeue -u username - Show user's jobs in queue
• sbatch job_script.sh - Submit batch job script to queue
• srun --pty bash - Get interactive shell on compute node
• sacct -j job_id --format=JobID,State,ExitCode - Check job status and exit code
• scontrol show job job_id - Display detailed job information
• sinfo -N -l - List all compute nodes and their status
• scancel job_id - Cancel or delete job from queue
• salloc --time=1:00:00 --nodes=1 - Allocate resources for interactive session

OPEN ONDEMAND:
• Access via web browser: https://cluster.domain.edu - Login to web portal
• Interactive Apps: Jupyter, RStudio, MATLAB, VSCode - Launch development environments
• File Manager: Upload/download files - Manage files through web interface
• Job Composer: Create and submit jobs - Build job scripts visually
• Active Jobs: Monitor running jobs - Track job progress and status
• Shell Access: Terminal access to compute nodes - Get command line access


[pbs_torque]
@title
PBS/Torque with Maui/Moab Scheduler

@summary
PBS (Portable Batch System) and Torque are job scheduling systems for HPC clusters, often paired with Maui or Moab schedulers for advanced scheduling policies. They provide job queuing, resource management, and workload distribution across compute nodes.

@challenges
• Job queue management and priority handling
• Resource allocation and scheduling optimization
• Job dependency and workflow management
• Fair share and user/group quotas
• Node failure handling and job recovery
• Performance monitoring and accounting
• Integration with storage and network systems

@commands
• qstat -u username - Show user's jobs in PBS queue
• qsub job_script.sh - Submit batch job script to PBS queue
• qdel job_id - Delete or cancel PBS job
• pbsnodes -a - List all compute nodes and their status
• showq -u username - Display user's jobs in Maui/Moab queue
• qstat -f job_id - Show detailed PBS job information
• qalter -l walltime=2:00:00 job_id - Modify job walltime to 2 hours
• qhold job_id - Hold job to prevent execution
• qrls job_id - Release held job to allow execution


[computational_jobs]
@title
Computational Job Management

@summary
Computational job management involves running, monitoring, and troubleshooting batch jobs, parallel computations, and distributed workloads. This includes job scheduling, resource allocation, performance optimization, and failure handling for scientific and engineering computations.

@challenges
• Job scheduling and resource allocation
• Performance optimization and bottleneck identification
• Memory management and out-of-memory errors
• I/O optimization and storage bottlenecks
• Parallel scaling and load balancing
• Job failure diagnosis and recovery
• Resource contention and queue management

@commands
• squeue -u username -o "%.18i %.9P %.8j %.8u %.2t %.10M %.6D %R"
• sacct -j job_id --format=JobID,State,ExitCode,Start,End,Elapsed
• scontrol show job job_id
• sinfo -N -l
• scancel job_id
• srun --ntasks=4 --cpus-per-task=2 ./program
• sbatch --time=2:00:00 --mem=8G job_script.sh


[programming]
@title
General Programming & Software Development

@summary
Programming involves writing, testing, and maintaining code to create software applications. It encompasses various programming languages, development methodologies, debugging techniques, and software engineering practices for building reliable and efficient applications.

@challenges
• Algorithm design and optimization
• Memory management and resource allocation
• Debugging complex logic errors
• Performance profiling and optimization
• Code maintainability and documentation
• Testing and quality assurance
• Version control and collaboration

@commands
• git init && git add . && git commit -m "Initial commit"
• gcc -o program source.c
• python -m py_compile script.py
• node --version && npm --version
• ruby -c script.rb
• bash -n script.sh
• lua -l script.lua
• powershell -Command "Get-Command"


[c_programming]
@title
C Programming Language

@summary
C is a low-level, compiled programming language known for its efficiency and direct hardware access. It's widely used in system programming, embedded systems, and performance-critical applications. C provides manual memory management and direct pointer manipulation.

@challenges
• Manual memory management and memory leaks
• Pointer arithmetic and segmentation faults
• Buffer overflows and security vulnerabilities
• Platform-specific code and portability
• Debugging complex pointer issues
• Performance optimization and profiling
• Cross-compilation and build systems

@commands
• gcc -Wall -Wextra -o program source.c
• gcc -g -o program source.c && gdb ./program
• valgrind --leak-check=full ./program
• make -f Makefile
• gcc -O2 -march=native -o program source.c
• objdump -d program
• nm program | grep function_name
• strace ./program


[javascript]
@title
JavaScript & Node.js Development

@summary
JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language primarily used for web development. Node.js extends JavaScript to server-side development. It features asynchronous programming, dynamic typing, and extensive ecosystem of libraries and frameworks.

@challenges
• Asynchronous programming and callback hell
• Memory leaks and garbage collection
• Cross-browser compatibility issues
• Performance optimization and bundle size
• Security vulnerabilities (XSS, CSRF)
• Debugging asynchronous code
• Dependency management and version conflicts

@commands
• node script.js
• npm init && npm install package
• npm run build
• yarn add package
• npx create-react-app my-app
• npm test
• node --inspect script.js
• npm audit && npm audit fix


[ruby]
@title
Ruby & Ruby on Rails

@summary
Ruby is a dynamic, object-oriented programming language known for its elegant syntax and developer productivity. Ruby on Rails is a web application framework that follows convention over configuration, providing rapid development capabilities for web applications.

@challenges
• Performance optimization and memory usage
• Database query optimization (N+1 problems)
• Security vulnerabilities and best practices
• Testing and test-driven development
• Deployment and production configuration
• Gem dependency management
• Scaling and performance monitoring

@commands
• ruby script.rb
• rails new myapp
• bundle install
• rails server
• rake db:migrate
• rails console
• rspec spec/
• bundle exec rubocop
• gem install gem_name


[shell_scripting]
@title
Shell Scripting (Bash/Zsh)

@summary
Shell scripting involves writing scripts using command-line interpreters like Bash or Zsh to automate system tasks, process data, and manage system operations. It's essential for system administration, DevOps, and automation workflows.

@challenges
• Portability across different shell environments
• Error handling and exit codes
• Variable scoping and subshell issues
• Performance optimization for large datasets
• Security vulnerabilities and input validation
• Debugging complex script logic
• Cross-platform compatibility

@commands
• bash script.sh
• chmod +x script.sh
• source ~/.bashrc
• export VARIABLE=value
• ps aux | grep process
• find /path -name "*.txt"
• awk '{print $1}' file.txt
• sed 's/old/new/g' file.txt
• cron -e


[lua]
@title
Lua Scripting Language

@summary
Lua is a lightweight, embeddable scripting language designed for extensibility and performance. It's commonly used in game development, embedded systems, and as an extension language for applications. Lua features coroutines, metatables, and a simple C API.

@challenges
• Memory management and garbage collection
• Performance optimization for embedded systems
• C API integration and binding
• Debugging embedded Lua scripts
• Error handling and exception management
• Threading and concurrency
• Cross-platform compatibility

@commands
• lua script.lua
• luajit script.lua
• lua -l module_name
• lua -e "print('Hello World')"
• lua -i (interactive mode)
• lua -v (version)
• lua -p script.lua (syntax check)
• lua -l debug script.lua


[powershell]
@title
PowerShell Scripting

@summary
PowerShell is a task automation and configuration management framework from Microsoft. It provides a command-line shell and scripting language built on .NET, with object-oriented capabilities and extensive system management features for Windows and cross-platform environments.

@challenges
• Execution policy and security restrictions
• Object pipeline and data manipulation
• Error handling and exception management
• Performance optimization for large datasets
• Cross-platform compatibility (PowerShell Core)
• Module management and dependencies
• Debugging complex scripts

@commands
• powershell -Command "Get-Process"
• powershell -File script.ps1
• Get-ExecutionPolicy
• Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned
• Import-Module ModuleName
• Get-Command -Module ModuleName
• Get-Help CommandName
• Test-Path "C:\path"

